WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:06.410
Netflix's Cowboy Bebop is like a live-action
jam session, riffing on the much-loved original

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:09.250
anime about a bunch of bounty hunters in space.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.430
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan, and
in this video I'm explaining the jazziest

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:16.420
differences between the new show and the animated
classic.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:22.280
At 49 years of age, John Cho may be over 20
years older than his character Spike Spiegel

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:26.869
was in the original anime, but what the actor
lacks in youth he more than makes up for with

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:28.690
acres of effortless cool.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:34.040
As the show's lead space cowboy Cho exudes
laid-back charisma and wry wit, breathing

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.660
entertaining life into Spike's often cynical
and cavalier approach to the world, while

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.150
also managing to tap into his darkness and
vulnerability.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:46.399
Interestingly, portraying vulnerability was
something Spike's English-language voice actor

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:49.470
in the anime, Steve Blum, said he found especially
difficult.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:54.010
And that's a benefit John Cho sees of coming
to the role as a more mature actor as he says

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:59.090
he's better now at "showing weakness and vulnerability
and love" so is more able to do "justice to

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.390
the emotional depth [they've] tried to give
Spike".

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:06.179
As for creating Spike's look, Cho trained
hard to become what he calls "functionally"

00:01:06.179 --> 00:01:10.429
rather than "show-horse athletic" to get a
good performance and also developed a "character

00:01:10.429 --> 00:01:14.549
walk" for the first time in his career, by
"watching how Spike moves in the original

00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:15.740
series".

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.729
And that hair is Cho's own grown out, rather
than a wig, which he was keen to avoid so

00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:25.539
it would feel and look more natural and wouldn't
pull on his scalp during the show's many action

00:01:25.539 --> 00:01:26.549
scenes.

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:30.740
Equally superb is Mustafa Shakir as former
cop Jet Black.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:35.039
Fans of Marvel's Luke Cage will doubtless
remember the actor as Bushmaster in season

00:01:35.039 --> 00:01:36.139
2 of that show.

00:01:36.139 --> 00:01:41.649
In Cowboy Bepop, Shakir brings his anime counterpart
to pitch-perfect life with a seen-it-all-before

00:01:41.649 --> 00:01:46.590
gruffness mixed with heart and humour, as
well as great on-screen chemistry with Cho.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:51.259
This time around, a young daughter's included
in Jet's backstory, adding a personal layer

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:54.270
to the professional hurt he suffered in his
past.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.229
While that's not really necessary to get us
on board with his character, it does lead

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:03.049
to a small but emotionally devastating moment
for him as well as some amusing moments along

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:07.420
the way, my favourite of which is him watching
his daughter's school performance in the foreground

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:12.720
totally oblivious to Spike fighting a slew
of baddies alone in the background.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.790
The third member of the Bebop's crew, Faye
Valentine, arrives on the scene a bit earlier

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:19.810
than in the anime;
and given Daniella Pineda's fantastic turn

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.610
as Faye, that's absolutely for the best.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:27.200
Pineda's take on the bold, brash amnesiac
brings a sense of wicked fun to the show whenever

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.550
she's on screen, whether she's bantering with
Spike and Jet or getting herself or others

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:36.160
into and out of crazy jams, like in the ridiculously
good seventh episode.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.380
With the odd exception, Faye doesn't keep
abandoning the team as her animated counterpart

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.550
often did, which improves the chemistry she
has with her fellow bounty hunters.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:49.620
In fact, all three lead actors work together
brilliantly, producing a truly entertaining

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:50.620
dynamic.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.900
Plus, Pineda imbues Faye's search for her
missing past with a quiet melancholy and it'll

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:59.130
be interesting to see how her hunt for answers
pans out in season 2.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:03.370
As for her appearance, obviously she was going
to look different in the real world and her

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:07.000
updated outfit reflects that while nodding
to the original.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.760
If you've watched Amazon's superhero series
The Boys, you'll likely recognise actor Alex

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.590
Hassell who played Translucent in that show
and stars as Vicious in Cowboy Bebop.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:20.860
How much you enjoy his turn here depends on
how theatrical you like your villains.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.170
Compared to the anime, we see much more of
Vicious in the live-action series.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:29.620
Given he's the show's main antagonist, building
up his character and expanding his backstory

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:35.350
makes sense as it makes him feel much more
obviously integral to the story from the get-go.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.400
But the penultimate episode that lays out
his journey to becoming a Syndicate capo and

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.920
his relationship with Spike is one of the
less interesting ones as it feels we've either

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.820
been told or intuited enough already, so it
ends up seeming like an overlong interruption

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:49.950
before the big finale.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:54.380
Oh, and if you're wondering what happened
to Vicious's pet cormorant from the anime,

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:59.020
the new show's creative team made an early
decision to give it the chop, which is understandable

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:02.990
given the added VFX costs to create something
that's not exactly crucial.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:07.340
However, they did include various easter eggs
to the bird on Vicious's costume.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:12.540
Julia, who is Vicious's wife and Spike's former
flame, also gets a lot more screen time than

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.540
she did in the original series where she was
more enigmatic.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:21.220
But she still mainly exists just to be part
of a love triangle, and actress Elena Satine

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:25.430
does what she can with a character who's mostly
there to appear fragile and sultry.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:30.020
Fortunately, there's a hint of more interesting
times ahead given where the show leaves her

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:34.110
character, and I'm intrigued to see where
the creators take things in Season 2.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:38.949
By the way, Satine gets to showcase her singing,
and according to John Cho, also plays the

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:44.300
piano herself in the series, performing a
new song that's important for Julia.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.651
Similar to the anime, Welsh corgi and "data
dog" Ein turns up in the lives of the Bebop

00:04:48.651 --> 00:04:51.800
crew thanks to a run-in with a pet napper
named Hakim.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:57.300
In the live-action series, two super-cute
canines, Harry and Charlie, bring Ein to absolutely

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:02.280
adorable life, though according to the show's
two male leads they were pretty heavy to carry

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.280
around!

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.749
As well as the real-life pups, John Cho says
he also spent a lot of time acting opposite

00:05:07.749 --> 00:05:11.909
a stuffed toy dog to get his eye alignment
right in scenes where Ein was off-screen.

00:05:11.909 --> 00:05:17.759
In terms of how the show's adapted overall,
it gets off to a delightfully playful, wacky

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:22.639
start with Spike and Jet at a casino on the
hunt for their latest criminal cash-cow.

00:05:22.639 --> 00:05:27.520
The scene is a joy to watch, from Spike's
elevator entrance and fancy fighting footwork

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.662
including a casino chip drop-kick, to Jet's
arrival crashlanding through the ceiling,

00:05:31.662 --> 00:05:35.240
to the duo's bickering, best-mates back-and-forth.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:39.770
Rather than simply remaking the original episodes
or creating an entirely different narrative

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:44.840
with the same core characters, the live-action
series aims to retain the anime's spirit and

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:48.090
takes a pick 'n' mix approach to its plots
and characters.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.979
In other words, they "get everybody and the
stuff together", but in a variety of ways.

00:05:51.979 --> 00:05:57.330
For instance, the first episode takes a similar
tack to the anime pilot, introducing Asimov

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.150
and Katerina and their attempted Red Eye drug
deal;

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.129
but in a change, it also introduces Faye.

00:06:03.129 --> 00:06:08.050
How the creative team rejigs or remixes elements
often works, but at times they don't quite

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:09.050
pull it off.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:13.469
For example, I was glad I already knew the
anime when I watched the ecoterroism episode

00:06:13.469 --> 00:06:18.039
with Maria Murdock because the live-action
take on that felt a bit too rushed to fully

00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:19.270
take in properly.

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:24.110
Each episode gives the Bebop crew fresh criminals
to bounty hunt, like in the anime.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:29.280
However, this version takes more regular time
from the start to expand on the crew's histories,

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.979
so those episodes feel less like standalone
stories and more clearly like part of a bigger,

00:06:34.979 --> 00:06:36.529
overarching narrative.

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:40.999
The series is packed with easter eggs and
hat-tips to the original, from building names

00:06:40.999 --> 00:06:42.580
to complete shots.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:47.819
And composer Yoko Kanno is back with new music
as well as old favourites, including her iconic

00:06:47.819 --> 00:06:51.259
opening theme tune, thank goodness because
I really love that!

00:06:51.259 --> 00:06:54.330
So, spoilers now for the Season 1 ending.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:58.860
The appearance of hacking whiz kid Radical
Edward was teased a few times this season.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.169
I've got a lead from Radical Ed.

00:07:00.169 --> 00:07:03.000
But Ed only appears right at the very end.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.110
Even though it's just a brief glimpse, we
can see this version has the signature look

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:10.930
and the hyper, childlike energy and smiley
eccentricity you'd expect.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.169
However, fan reactions have been mixed.

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:17.820
with some suggesting that Ed's anime oddness
doesn't translate well to live action.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:22.580
We'll have to wait for Season 2 to see exactly
how the character plays out, but I wonder

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:27.259
whether Radical Ed will hack into the Bebop
to force Jet to come and join her, Spike and

00:07:27.259 --> 00:07:30.570
Ein, similar to how she hijacked the ship
in the anime.

